<BaseLayout
  title="Custom Peptide Synthesis | Shengyuan Peptide"
  description="Custom peptide synthesis from milligrams to kilograms — sequence review, modifications, HPLC purification, MS identity and batch-specific documentation."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap article-shell">
    <div class="article-header">
      <h1>Custom Peptide Synthesis</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        From a sequence file to a released batch: feasibility review, synthesis, purification,
        analytics and documentation. Research milligrams to multi-gram and kilogram programs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article>
      <p>
        Custom peptide synthesis is a specification-driven service, not a catalogue order. The
        sequence, scale, purity target, salt form, modification set and documentation requirements
        all change the chemistry and the analytical work behind a batch. A useful quotation therefore
        starts with a review of the sequence and the intended use — not with a price per milligram.
      </p>

      <p>
        This page explains what our synthesis platform covers, how a project moves from inquiry to
        release, and which details a buyer should settle before the first coupling. All products
        described here are research materials supplied for laboratory use only. They are not
        medicinal products and are not intended for human or veterinary use.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-we-synthesize">What Custom Synthesis Covers</h2>
      <p>
        The platform is built around solid-phase peptide synthesis (SPPS), with purification and
        analytical release configured around the target sequence. Typical projects include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Linear peptides</strong> — standard sequences, long chains and hydrophobic or aggregation-prone regions.</li>
        <li><strong>Cyclic peptides</strong> — head-to-tail, disulfide-bridged and stapled structures.</li>
        <li><strong>Modified peptides</strong> — N-terminal acetylation, C-terminal amidation, D-amino acid substitution, non-natural residues and terminal labels.</li>
        <li><strong>Conjugates</strong> — PEGylation, biotinylation, fluorescent dyes and carrier-protein conjugation.</li>
        <li><strong>Isotopically labelled peptides</strong> — where the label position and enrichment are specified.</li>
        <li><strong>Salt forms</strong> — acetate, TFA and other counterions by agreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Scale-up</strong> — from feasibility milligrams to pilot and larger production batches.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="feasibility">Sequence Feasibility Review</h2>
      <p>
        The first technical step is a review of the sequence, not a promise that every sequence will
        behave the same way. The review considers length, hydrophobicity, charge distribution,
        aggregation-prone motifs, cysteine content, proline-rich regions and residues that are slow to
        couple. It also considers the requested purity, quantity, salt form and modification set.
      </p>
      <p>
        A realistic feasibility response should tell you:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>whether the sequence is routine, difficult or uncertain;</li>
        <li>which synthesis strategy is proposed;</li>
        <li>what purity grade is realistic for the sequence;</li>
        <li>which analytical methods will be used;</li>
        <li>the expected timeline and the assumptions behind it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If a supplier quotes every sequence at the same purity and the same lead time without asking
        about the sequence, the quotation is not yet a technical plan. Our
        <a href="/knowledge-base/custom-peptide-synthesis-guide/">sequence-to-delivery guide</a>
        explains the chemistry in more detail.
      </p>

      <h2 id="chemistry">The Synthesis Chemistry</h2>
      <p>
        Most custom peptides are produced by Fmoc/tBu solid-phase synthesis. The C-terminal residue is
        anchored to a resin, and the chain is extended residue by residue. Each cycle includes
        deprotection, washing, activation and coupling, followed by capping of unreacted chains so
        they do not become deletion sequences in the final product.
      </p>
      <p>
        Difficult sequences may require double coupling, elevated temperature, microwave assistance,
        pseudoproline dipeptides, backbone protection, lower resin loading or fragment strategies.
        The choice is sequence-specific. A supplier should be able to explain why a particular
        strategy was selected, not only report that the synthesis was “successful”.
      </p>
      <p>
        After chain assembly, the peptide is cleaved from the resin and the side-chain protecting
        groups are removed. The crude material is then purified, usually by preparative reverse-phase
        HPLC, and the fractions are checked before pooling.
      </p>

      <h2 id="modifications">Modifications and Conjugates</h2>
      <p>
        Modifications are not cosmetic. They change mass, charge, hydrophobicity, stability and the
        analytical method needed to confirm identity. Common requests include:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Modification</th><th>Typical purpose</th><th>Analytical check</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>N-terminal acetylation</td><td>Block the N-terminus; reduce charge</td><td>Mass shift and sequence coverage</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C-terminal amidation</td><td>Remove the terminal acid; adjust charge</td><td>Mass shift and terminal fragment</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Disulfide cyclization</td><td>Constrain conformation</td><td>Mass, reduced/oxidized forms, HPLC profile</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D-amino acid substitution</td><td>Change proteolytic stability or conformation</td><td>Mass, retention time, sequence confirmation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PEGylation</td><td>Increase hydrodynamic size; alter clearance in research models</td><td>Conjugate mass distribution and purity</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Biotinylation</td><td>Capture or detection</td><td>Mass shift and biotin functional check</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fluorescent labelling</td><td>Imaging or binding assays</td><td>Dye-to-peptide ratio and mass</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Each modification should be written into the specification, including the expected mass and
        the position of the label. The
        <a href="/knowledge-base/custom-peptide-modifications/">custom modifications guide</a>
        covers the practical differences and the questions to ask before ordering.
      </p>

      <h2 id="purification">Purification and Analytical Release</h2>
      <p>
        A synthesis result is not a released batch until the analytical package supports the
        specification. Typical release tests include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Appearance</strong> — colour and cake integrity of the lyophilised powder;</li>
        <li><strong>Identity</strong> — ESI-MS or MALDI-TOF against the calculated mass;</li>
        <li><strong>Purity</strong> — RP-HPLC area percentage at a stated wavelength and method;</li>
        <li><strong>Peptide content</strong> — net peptide content, not only chromatographic purity;</li>
        <li><strong>Water content</strong> — Karl Fischer where relevant;</li>
        <li><strong>Counterion / salt form</strong> — acetate, TFA or other agreed form;</li>
        <li><strong>Endotoxin</strong> — where the research program requires a stated limit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Purity is method-dependent. A “≥99%” figure should always be read with the HPLC method,
        wavelength, integration rules and sample preparation. The
        <a href="/quality/">quality page</a> describes the release panel; the
        <a href="/coa/">COA page</a> explains what a batch certificate records.
      </p>

      <h2 id="scale">Scale and Turnaround</h2>
      <p>
        Scale changes the project, not just the quantity. A milligram feasibility batch and a
        multi-gram production batch may use different resin loading, purification and lyophilisation
        conditions.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Typical scope</th><th>Planning note</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Feasibility</td><td>Milligram quantities</td><td>Used to confirm synthesis and analytical behaviour</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Research batch</td><td>1–100 mg</td><td>Sequence and purity determine the timeline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pilot batch</td><td>1–50 g</td><td>Process and analytical parameters are fixed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Larger production</td><td>50 g and above</td><td>Project plan, batch records and delivery schedule agreed in writing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Lead time is sequence- and queue-dependent. A standard sequence may be routine; a difficult
        or heavily modified sequence may require optimization before a reliable delivery date can be
        confirmed. Treat a quotation without assumptions as provisional.
      </p>

      <h2 id="documentation">Documentation and Batch Release</h2>
      <p>
        For procurement, the analytical result and the batch record are separate questions. The
        certificate states what was tested and what was found. The batch record shows how the batch
        was made, reviewed and released. A complete documentation package should allow a buyer to
        connect:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the product identity and sequence;</li>
        <li>the batch or lot identifier;</li>
        <li>the analytical methods and results;</li>
        <li>the specification and acceptance criteria;</li>
        <li>the release decision and date;</li>
        <li>the storage and retest information.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Additional documents — quality dossier, raw HPLC/MS data, method summaries or change-control
        records — should be requested during supplier qualification, not after a problem appears.
        See the <a href="/knowledge-base/peptide-quality-documentation/">quality documentation guide</a>
        for the document set buyers commonly request.
      </p>

      <h2 id="confidentiality">Sequence Confidentiality and Project Boundaries</h2>
      <p>
        Custom sequences can be commercially sensitive. Before sending a sequence, confirm:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>who will see the sequence and the project details;</li>
        <li>whether a confidentiality agreement is required;</li>
        <li>how long project records are retained;</li>
        <li>whether the supplier will use the sequence for any other purpose;</li>
        <li>who owns the project data and any resulting materials.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A clear project boundary is part of the technical service. It is not an administrative
        afterthought.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rfq">What to Include in an RFQ</h2>
      <p>
        A complete request shortens the feasibility review and reduces revision cycles. Include:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>the full sequence, including unusual residues and terminal groups;</li>
        <li>the required quantity and number of aliquots;</li>
        <li>the target purity and the analytical method you expect;</li>
        <li>the salt form and any counterion requirement;</li>
        <li>the modification set and expected mass;</li>
        <li>the required documentation package;</li>
        <li>the destination country and any shipping constraints;</li>
        <li>the target date and whether it is fixed or flexible.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If you do not yet know the right purity or scale, say so. A feasibility discussion is more
        useful than a guessed specification.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Can you synthesize peptides longer than 50 residues?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes, but long sequences are reviewed individually. Fragment strategies or alternative
        conditions may be required, and the feasible purity and timeline depend on the sequence.
      </p>
      <h3>Is ≥99% purity available for every peptide?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Purity grades are sequence- and method-dependent. A high-purity target may be realistic
        for a routine sequence and difficult for an aggregation-prone or heavily modified one. The
        specification should match the analytical method and the intended use.
      </p>
      <h3>Can you keep my sequence confidential?</h3>
      <p>
        Confidentiality terms can be agreed before a sequence is shared. Discuss the scope, record
        retention and project data ownership with the technical contact.
      </p>
      <h3>Do you provide a batch-specific COA?</h3>
      <p>
        The release package is built around the batch. The certificate fields and the available raw
        data should be confirmed for the specific project and specification.
      </p>
      <h3>Can you handle OEM and private-label projects?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. OEM projects add packaging, labelling, quality-agreement and responsibility-split
        requirements. See the <a href="/oem/">OEM page</a> for the collaboration model.
      </p>

      <h2 id="next-step">Next Step</h2>
      <p>
        Send the sequence, quantity, purity target, modification set and required documentation to
        <a href="/contact/">our technical team</a>. We will review feasibility and respond with the
        assumptions behind the quotation. If you are still defining the specification, start with the
        <a href="/knowledge-base/custom-peptide-synthesis-guide/">sequence-to-delivery guide</a> or the
        <a href="/knowledge-base/custom-peptide-modifications/">modifications guide</a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Research use only.</strong> Custom peptides supplied by Shandong Shengyuan Peptide
      Technology Co., Ltd. are for laboratory research. They are not medicines, not for human or
      veterinary use, and not for diagnostic or therapeutic applications.
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
